==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|inocence|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|innocentia|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɪnəsn̩s/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[absence|Absence]] of [[responsibility]] for a [[crime]].
#: {{usex|Her attorney managed to convince the jury of her '''innocence'''.|lang=en}}
# [[lack|Lack]] of [[understanding]] about sensitive subjects such as sexuality and crime.
#* {{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=9|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Eustace gaped at him in amazement. When his urbanity dropped away from him, as now, he had an '''innocence''' of expression which was almost infantile. It was as if the world had never touched him at all.}}
#: {{usex|In his '''innocence''', he offered the stranger to bring the package to Paris, never suspecting it contained drugs.|lang=en}}
# [[lack|Lack]] of [[ability]] or [[intention]] to [[harm]] or damage.
#: {{usex|Tests have demonstrated the '''innocence''' of this substance.|lang=en}}

====Antonyms====
* {{sense|absence of responsibility for a crime}} [[guilt]]
* {{sense|absence of ability to harm}} [[harmfulness]]

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|lack of sensitive subjects}} [[naivety]]
* {{sense|absence of ability to harm}} [[harmlessness]]
* {{sense|freedom from guilt}} [[unguilt]], [[unguiltness]]

====Related terms====
* [[innocent]]

